@*
    CascadingAuthenticationState supplies the circuit's Task<AuthenticationState> — in Blazor Server that is
    seeded from the authenticated user of the connection that opened the circuit — to everything below it,
    which is what lets AuthenticationBoundary and AuthorizeRouteView ask who the caller is at all.

    Two gates sit between a route and its page, and they answer different questions. AuthenticationBoundary
    is the default-deny one: no page reaches AuthorizeRouteView unless the caller is authenticated (or the
    route is explicitly [AllowAnonymous], or authentication is switched off wholesale). AuthorizeRouteView is
    the per-page one: it honours any [Authorize] policy a page declares. Neither is the real gate — that is
    the FallbackPolicy in Program.cs, which refuses the HTTP request before a component exists — but an
    in-circuit navigation makes no HTTP request, so these are what stand in its place there.
*@
<CascadingAuthenticationState>
    <Router AppAssembly="typeof(Program).Assembly" NotFoundPage="typeof(Pages.NotFound)">
        <Found Context="routeData">
            <AuthenticationBoundary RouteData="routeData">
                <AuthorizeRouteView RouteData="routeData" DefaultLayout="typeof(Layout.MainLayout)">
                    <NotAuthorized>
                        <RedirectToLogin />
                    </NotAuthorized>
                </AuthorizeRouteView>
                <FocusOnNavigate RouteData="routeData" Selector="h1" />
            </AuthenticationBoundary>
        </Found>
    </Router>
</CascadingAuthenticationState>
